.TH RM 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
rm \- remove directory entries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rm
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-r | \-R ]
.I file
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rm
removes the directory entry named by each
.I file
operand. Multiple operands are processed in turn; an error on one
operand does not stop the rest from being attempted.
.PP
A
.I file
operand that names a directory is refused with a diagnostic unless
.B \-r
or
.B \-R
is also given, in which case the whole tree rooted at that directory
is removed, deepest entries first. The tree walk never follows
symbolic links found inside it: a symbolic link encountered during the
walk is removed as itself, not the file it refers to.
.PP
.B rm
refuses to remove a
.I file
operand whose final pathname component is exactly
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
by name, before touching the filesystem at all; this refusal applies
even when
.B \-f
is given. It does not otherwise attempt to recognize a pathname that
names a filesystem root; an attempt to remove an actual root is left
to fail on its own through the same removal call any other directory
would use, and is diagnosed like any other removal failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Do not write a diagnostic message, and do not affect the exit status,
for a
.I file
operand that does not exist. This is the only thing
.B \-f
suppresses: it does not apply to a
.I file
operand that names a directory when
.BR \-r / \-R
was not given, and it does not apply to any other removal failure
(such as a permission error, or a non-empty directory that cannot be
removed). It also does not apply to the complete absence of any
.I file
operand; invoking
.B rm
with no operands at all is always an error, with or without
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R
Remove file hierarchies: if a
.I file
operand names a directory, remove its entire contents before removing
the directory itself, instead of refusing the operand. The two forms
are exact synonyms.
.TP
.B \-i
Not implemented.
.B rm
prints a diagnostic and exits immediately with status 2 rather than
delete anything without ever having prompted for confirmation.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of a directory entry to remove.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was removed successfully (or did not exist, under
.BR \-f ).
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred while removing one or more operands; the rest of the
operands were still attempted.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid option, or no
.I file
operand at all. No removal is attempted in this case.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rmdir (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR mv (1)
